@subsubheading Parsing natural language

Programs designed to accept natural language as input usually start by
attempting to @newterm{parse} the input, that is, to match the input against
some grammatical structure.  For example, we might try to recognize simple
sentences consisting of an article followed by a noun followed by a verb, such
as ``The cat eats.''  To accomplish such an analysis, we must be able to
identify the parts of speech of individual words.  We could start with some
lists that classify various words:@footnote{Here we use the convention that the
first component of each pair names the part of speech of the words in the
list.}

@example ocaml
let nouns = ("noun", [ "student"; "professor"; "cat"; "class" ])
let verbs = ("verb", [ "studies"; "lectures"; "eats"; "sleeps" ])
let articles = ("article", [ "the"; "a" ])
@end example

@noindent
We also need a @newterm{grammar}, that is, a set of rules describing how
grammatical elements are composed from simpler elements.  A very simple grammar
might stipulate that a sentence always consists of two pieces, a noun phrase
followed by a verb, and that a noun phrase consists of an article followed by
a noun.  With this grammar, the sentence ``The cat eats'' is parsed as follows:

@example
(sentence
 (noun-phrase (article the) (noun cat))
 (verb eats))
@end example

@noindent
A parse is a tree, which we represent with a variant type, and the printed
form above is what the procedure @code{show} makes of it:

@example ocaml
type tree =
  | Leaf of string * string
  | Node of string * tree list

let rec show t =
  match t with
  | Leaf (category, word) -> "(" ^ category ^ " " ^ word ^ ")"
  | Node (category, parts) -> "(" ^ category ^ show_all parts ^ ")"

and show_all parts =
  match parts with
  | [] -> ""
  | p :: rest -> " " ^ show p ^ show_all rest
@end example

@noindent
We can generate such a parse with a simple program that has separate procedures
for each of the grammatical rules.  To parse a sentence, we identify its two
constituent pieces and return a node of these two elements, tagged with the
category @code{sentence}:

@example ocaml
let parse_sentence _ =
  Node ("sentence", [ parse_noun_phrase (); parse_word verbs ])
@end example

@noindent
A noun phrase, similarly, is parsed by finding an article followed by a
noun:

@example ocaml
let parse_noun_phrase _ =
  Node ("noun-phrase", [ parse_word articles; parse_word nouns ])
@end example

@noindent
At the lowest level, parsing boils down to repeatedly checking that the next
unparsed word is a member of the list of words for the required part of speech.
To implement this, we maintain a global reference @code{unparsed}, which holds
the input that has not yet been parsed.  Each time we check a word, we require
that @code{unparsed} must be non-empty and that it should begin with a word
from the designated list.  If so, we remove that word from @code{unparsed}
and return the word together with its part of speech:@footnote{Notice that
@code{parse_word} uses @code{:=} to modify the unparsed input list.  For this
to work, our @code{amb} evaluator must undo the effects of assignments when it
backtracks.  @ref{4.3.3} shows how this edition's evaluator does it.}

@example ocaml
let unparsed = ref []

let parse_word word_list =
  match word_list, !unparsed with
  | (category, _), [] -> require false; Leaf (category, "")
  | (category, words), word :: rest ->
    require
      (List.length (List.filter (fun w -> w = word) words) > 0);
    unparsed := rest;
    Leaf (category, word)
@end example

@noindent
To start the parsing, all we need to do is set @code{unparsed} to be the
entire input, try to parse a sentence, and check that nothing is left over:

@example ocaml
let parse input =
  unparsed := input;
  let sent = parse_sentence () in
  require (match !unparsed with [] -> true | _ -> false);
  sent
@end example

@noindent
In the program, these declarations come in the order that lets each one see
the procedures it uses: @code{unparsed} and @code{parse_word} first, then
@code{parse_noun_phrase}, @code{parse_sentence}, and @code{parse}.  We can now
try the parser and verify that it works for our simple test sentence:

@example ocaml
let () = print_endline (show (parse [ "the"; "cat"; "eats" ]))
@end example

@noindent
prints

@example
(sentence (noun-phrase (article the) (noun cat)) (verb eats))
@end example

@noindent
The @code{amb} evaluator is useful here because it is convenient to express the
parsing constraints with the aid of @code{require}.  Automatic search and
backtracking really pay off, however, when we consider more complex grammars
where there are choices for how the units can be decomposed.

Let's add to our grammar a list of prepositions:

@example ocaml
let prepositions = ("prep", [ "for"; "to"; "in"; "by"; "with" ])
@end example

@noindent
and define a prepositional phrase (e.g., ``for the cat'') to be a preposition
followed by a noun phrase:

@example ocaml
and parse_prepositional_phrase _ =
  Node ("prep-phrase",
        [ parse_word prepositions; parse_noun_phrase () ])
@end example

@noindent
Now we can define a sentence to be a noun phrase followed by a verb phrase,
where a verb phrase can be either a verb or a verb phrase extended by a
prepositional phrase:@footnote{Observe that this definition is recursive: a
verb may be followed by any number of prepositional phrases.}

@example ocaml
let parse_verb_phrase _ =
  let rec maybe_extend verb_phrase =
    amb verb_phrase
      (maybe_extend
         (Node ("verb-phrase",
                [ verb_phrase; parse_prepositional_phrase () ])))
  in
  maybe_extend (parse_word verbs)

let parse_sentence _ =
  Node ("sentence", [ parse_noun_phrase (); parse_verb_phrase () ])
@end example

@noindent
While we're at it, we can also elaborate the definition of noun phrases to
permit such things as ``a cat in the class.''  What we used to call a noun
phrase, we'll now call a simple noun phrase, and a noun phrase will now be
either a simple noun phrase or a noun phrase extended by a prepositional
phrase.  A noun phrase and a prepositional phrase now refer to each other, so
they are one recursive group, which the @code{and parse_prepositional_phrase}
above completes:

@example ocaml
let rec parse_simple_noun_phrase _ =
  Node ("simple-noun-phrase",
        [ parse_word articles; parse_word nouns ])

and parse_noun_phrase _ =
  let rec maybe_extend noun_phrase =
    amb noun_phrase
      (maybe_extend
         (Node ("noun-phrase",
                [ noun_phrase; parse_prepositional_phrase () ])))
  in
  maybe_extend (parse_simple_noun_phrase ())
@end example

@noindent
Our new grammar lets us parse more complex sentences.  For example, parsing
the words of ``the student with the cat sleeps in the class'' prints (with
line breaks added)

@example
(sentence
 (noun-phrase
  (simple-noun-phrase (article the) (noun student))
  (prep-phrase (prep with)
               (simple-noun-phrase (article the) (noun cat))))
 (verb-phrase
  (verb sleeps)
  (prep-phrase (prep in)
               (simple-noun-phrase (article the) (noun class)))))
@end example

@noindent
Observe that a given input may have more than one legal parse.  In the sentence
``The professor lectures to the student with the cat,'' it may be that the
professor is lecturing with the cat, or that the student has the cat.  Our
nondeterministic program finds both possibilities.  The first parse it prints
is

@example
(sentence
 (simple-noun-phrase (article the) (noun professor))
 (verb-phrase
  (verb-phrase
   (verb lectures)
   (prep-phrase (prep to)
                (simple-noun-phrase (article the) (noun student))))
  (prep-phrase (prep with)
               (simple-noun-phrase (article the) (noun cat)))))
@end example

@noindent
and the search goes on to print the second:

@example
(sentence
 (simple-noun-phrase (article the) (noun professor))
 (verb-phrase
  (verb lectures)
  (prep-phrase (prep to)
               (noun-phrase
                (simple-noun-phrase (article the) (noun student))
                (prep-phrase (prep with)
                             (simple-noun-phrase
                              (article the) (noun cat)))))))
@end example

@noindent
The driver then reports @code{answers: 2}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.45}Exercise 4.45:} With the grammar given above, the
following sentence can be parsed in five different ways: ``The professor
lectures to the student in the class with the cat.''  Give the five parses and
explain the differences in shades of meaning among them.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.46}Exercise 4.46:} Native OCaml does not determine
what order the elements of a list literal or the operands of an application
are evaluated in.  The @code{amb} evaluator, like every teaching evaluator of
this edition, evaluates them from left to right.  Explain why our parsing
