appropriate word, we can use the programs we had built for parsing to do
generation instead.  Implement Alyssa's idea, and show the first half-dozen or
so sentences generated.@footnote{Although Alyssa's idea works just fine (and is
surprisingly simple), the sentences that it generates are a bit boring: they
don't sample the possible sentences of this language in a very interesting way.
In fact, the grammar is highly recursive in many places, and Alyssa's technique
``falls into'' one of these recursions and gets stuck.  See @ref{Exercise 4.50}
for a way to deal with this.}
@end quotation

@node	4.3.3, 4.4, 4.2, 4.3
@subsection Implementing the @code{Amb} Evaluator

The evaluation of an ordinary expression may return a value, may never
terminate, or may signal an error.  In the nondeterministic language the
evaluation of an expression may in addition result in the discovery of a dead
end, in which case evaluation must backtrack to a previous choice point.  The
interpretation of the nondeterministic language is complicated by this extra
case.

The original book builds its @code{amb} evaluator from the analyzing evaluator
of @ref{4.1.7}.  Its execution procedures take two continuation procedures, a
@newterm{success continuation} that receives a value and a @newterm{failure
continuation} that tries another branch, and backtracking is a call of the
failure continuation.  This edition takes a different route to the same
search, one that keeps the evaluator of @ref{4.1.1} almost unchanged: it
@emph{restarts} the program.@footnote{The grammar of this edition allows the
host to use OCaml 5 effect handlers for the search, with the rule that a
captured continuation is never resumed more than once.  A continuation that
backtracking must resume again for the next alternative would break that rule,
so the evaluator replays the program instead of resuming it.  @ref{Exercise
4.50} to @ref{Exercise 4.54} extend this evaluator by open recursion.}

@subsubheading Paths of choices

A run of the program is determined by the alternatives it takes at its choice
points.  We represent a branch of the search by the list of those choices, one
index per @code{amb} in the order the run reaches them, and call it a
@newterm{path}.  One @newterm{attempt} runs the whole program deterministically
along a path.  An attempt ends in one of four ways: it completes, which is a
successful execution; it fails at a @code{require}; it reaches a choice point
beyond the end of its path; or it stops on a runtime error, which ends the
whole search:

@example ocaml
type stop =
  | Failed
  | Incomplete of int
  | Broke of Eval_error.t

type 'a step = ('a, stop) result
@end example

@noindent
The evaluator is the evaluator of @ref{4.1.1} with @code{step} in place of
@code{result}.  It receives the attempt's @code{search} state, which holds the
decisions still to take and the decisions taken so far.  A choice point takes
the next decision.  When the path is used up, the attempt stops with
@code{Incomplete n}, where @code{n} is the number of alternatives of the
choice point:

@example ocaml
let choose search n =
  if n <= 0
  then type_error "a choice point needs at least one alternative"
  else (
    match search.decisions with
    | decision :: rest when decision >= 0 && decision < n ->
      search.decisions <- rest;
      search.taken <- decision :: search.taken;
      Ok decision
    | _ :: _ -> fail
    | [] -> Error (Incomplete n))
@end example

@subsubheading Choice points and requirements

The two new special forms are recognized at the head of an application by
their names.  @code{Amb} takes a decision and evaluates only the alternative
it selects.  @code{Require} evaluates its condition and fails the attempt when
the condition is false.  @code{Require} is a second clause of the same shape: it evaluates its
condition and fails the attempt when the condition is false (see
@ref{Exercise 4.54}).  Every other application is evaluated as in
@ref{4.1.1}:

@example ocaml
and apply_from_expr self state env fn args =
  match Ast.view fn with
  | Ast.Var "amb" ->
    let* decision = choose state (List.length args) in
    self state env (List.nth args decision)
  | _ ->
    let* fn = self state env fn in
    let* args = eval_list self state env args in
    apply_value self state fn args
@end example

@subsubheading Assignments

If a side-effect operation (such as assignment to a reference) occurs on a
branch of the process resulting from a choice, it may be necessary, when the
process finds a dead end, to undo the side effect before making a new choice.
The restarting evaluator gets this for free.  Every attempt runs the program
from the beginning, so it builds its own references, and an assignment made by
a failed attempt is gone with the attempt.  The output of an attempt goes to a
buffer of its own, and the driver copies the buffer to the real output only if
the attempt completes.@footnote{We don't need to worry about undoing
@code{let} bindings, since the environments of an attempt are built afresh by
the attempt.}  An assignment that should survive backtracking, the
@code{permanent_set} of @ref{Exercise 4.51}, must therefore be recorded outside
the attempts, in a store that the driver keeps for the whole search.

@subsubheading The driver

The driver keeps a list of the paths still to try, starting with the empty
path.  It runs an attempt for the first path.  A completed attempt is an
answer, and its output is printed.  A failed attempt is dropped.  An attempt
that reaches a choice point past the end of its path extends the search: the
driver puts one new path for each alternative of that choice point at the
front of the list, lowest index first, which makes the search depth-first and
tries the alternatives from left to right:

@example ocaml
let drive ~eval ~emit program =
  let store =
    @{ survived = Hashtbl.create 8; applied = Hashtbl.create 8 @}
  in
  let rec loop pending answers choices failures =
    match pending with
    | [] -> Ok (answers, choices, failures)
    | path :: rest ->
      let state =
        @{ decisions = path; taken = []; permanents = ref 0;
          top = []; store @}
      in
      let branch = Buffer.create 128 in
      (match
         attempt eval state ~emit:(Buffer.add_string branch) program
       with
       | Ok _ ->
         emit (Buffer.contents branch);
         loop rest (answers + 1) choices failures
       | Error Failed -> loop rest answers choices (failures + 1)
       | Error (Incomplete arity) ->
         let branches = List.init arity (fun i -> path @@ [ i ]) in
         loop (branches @@ rest) answers (choices + 1) failures
       | Error (Broke e) -> Error e)
  in
  @r{…}
@end example

@noindent
When the list of paths is empty, the driver prints the three counts.  A
restarting search repeats the work of the common prefix of two paths, so it
does more work than the book's continuation-passing evaluator on the same
program.  In return, the search order is explicit data, a list of paths, which
the exercises inspect and change.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.50}Exercise 4.50:} Implement a new special form
@code{ramb} that is like @code{amb} except that it searches alternatives in a
random order, rather than from left to right.  Show how this can help with
Alyssa's problem in @ref{Exercise 4.49}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.51}Exercise 4.51:} Implement a new kind of
assignment called @code{permanent_set} that is not undone upon failure.  For
example, we can choose two distinct elements from a list and count the number
of trials required to make a successful choice as follows:

@example
let count = ref 0

let () =
  let x = an_element_of [ "a"; "b"; "c" ] in
  let y = an_element_of [ "a"; "b"; "c" ] in
  permanent_set count (!count + 1);
  require (x <> y);
  print_endline (x ^ " " ^ y ^ " " ^ string_of_int !count)
@end example

@noindent
The first two lines the program prints are

@example
a b 2
a c 3
@end example

What values would have been displayed if we had used @code{:=} here
rather than @code{permanent_set}?  The experiment admits the new form with
a declaration, as it admits @code{amb} and @code{require}:

@example ocaml
let permanent_set r v = r := v
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.52}Exercise 4.52:} Implement a new construct called
@code{if_fail} that permits the user to catch the failure of an expression.
@code{If_fail} takes two expressions.  It evaluates the first expression as
usual and returns as usual if the evaluation succeeds.  If the evaluation
fails, however, the value of the second expression is returned, as in the
following example:

@example
let () =
  print_endline
    (if_fail
       (let x = an_element_of [ 1; 3; 5 ] in
        require (even x);
        string_of_int x)
       "all-odd")
@end example

@noindent
prints @code{all-odd}, and the same program with the list
@code{[ 1; 3; 5; 8 ]} prints @code{8}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.53}Exercise 4.53:} With @code{permanent_set} as
described in @ref{Exercise 4.51} and @code{if_fail} as in @ref{Exercise 4.52},
what will be the result of evaluating

@example
let pairs = ref []

let () =
  show_pairs
    (if_fail
       (let p =
          prime_sum_pair
            [ 1; 3; 5; 8 ] [ 20; 35; 110 ]
        in
        permanent_set pairs (p :: !pairs);
        require false;
        !pairs)
       !pairs)
@end example

@noindent
where @code{show_pairs} prints a list of pairs?
@end quotation

